<div id="2026-09-11-inventory-sku-uniqueness-guard" class="cl-entry">
<h3>2026-09-11 — Inventory SKU uniqueness hardened (no duplicate SKU per site)</h3>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>What.</strong> Closed the gap that let a duplicate SKU get created (root cause of the HDRY BBL01 triple-row in the traveler: item 174 "Board Cap" was mislabeled <code>INT-HDRY-BBL01</code>, colliding with the real BBL01 id 59). Three layers now enforce <strong>(sitename, sku) uniqueness</strong>:</p>
<ul class="doc-ul">
  <li><strong>Schema result class</strong> (<code>InventoryItem.pm</code>): changed the unique constraint from single-column <code>['sku']</code> to <code>['sitename','sku']</code> — a SKU may still exist on different sites (CSC vs 3d) but never twice in one site.</li>
  <li><strong>App guard in <code>_create_item</code></strong> (the single creation entry point for the web <code>item_add</code> form AND the AI/3d <code>api_item_create</code> API): before <code>create</code>, it does <code>find({sitename, sku})</code> and, if found, logs a warn and returns the existing row instead of creating a twin. This is the direct fix for "AI used a script and not the API" — any create now funnels through this check.</li>
  <li><strong>Update guard in <code>api_item_update</code></strong>: rejects (HTTP 409) any SKU change that would collide with another item in the same site, so edits can't manufacture a duplicate either.</li>
  <li><strong>Cross-site fix in <code>api_item_create</code></strong>: its own pre-check previously searched bare <code>sku</code> globally and could return a different-site item as "existed"; now scoped to <code>(sitename, sku)</code> to match the new rule.</li>
</ul>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Why.</strong> A duplicate SKU corrupts BOM explosion and traveler display: the traveler dedupes by <code>item_id</code>, not SKU, so two items sharing a SKU render as separate rows (BBL01 appeared 3×). The duplicate was almost certainly written outside the app (script/raw insert), bypassing any in-app check. Hardening at the app layer makes the app the sole safe path.</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Files.</strong> <code>lib/Comserv/Controller/Inventory.pm</code> (<code>_create_item</code> guard, <code>api_item_update</code> guard, <code>api_item_create</code> scope fix), <code>lib/Comserv/Model/Schema/Ency/Result/Accounting/InventoryItem.pm</code> (constraint). <code>perl -c</code> passes on all three.</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>REMAINING (schema branch, not done here).</strong> DBIC <code>add_unique_constraint</code> prevents duplicates created *through DBIC* but does NOT create a database-level UNIQUE INDEX, so a raw <code>INSERT</code> (the original failure mode) can still slip past. The actual <code>UNIQUE INDEX (sitename, sku)</code> must be added via the schema-compare / migration flow on the <code>schema</code> branch — no hand-DDL per project rules. Also: the existing duplicate (item 174) must be corrected to <code>INT-HDRY-BOARD-CAP</code> via the app edit UI/API to clear the current traveler symptom.</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>CODER_READY.</strong> If a future task needs to also enforce at the DB, open a schema-branch work order for the unique index; do not hand-write SQL.</p>
</div>
